<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis II of Italy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 825–875</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Louis II, Holy Roman Emperor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a>, king of Italy from 844 and Carolingian emperor from 855. When his father's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a> was divided he received Italy, and he shared Provence with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>. He spent much of his reign fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in southern Italy and captured their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> in 871.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy.jpg" alt="Louis II of Italy" width="237" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes Berardi (chronicon casauriense) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_II_of_Italy.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis was born in 825, the eldest son of the junior emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> and Ermengarde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. He grew up in the court of his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, who in 839 designated him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. After Louis the Pious died the next year the empire was partitioned between Lothair, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, and on 15 June 844 Pope Sergius II crowned Louis king and co-emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His first claim to imperial rights in the city was decisively rejected. In 850 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iv">Pope Leo IV</a> crowned him joint emperor, and in 851 he married Engelberga and took on the independent government of Italy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 850 Louis marched into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and compelled the rival dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, Radelchis I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siconulf">Siconulf</a>, to make peace, splitting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> duchy between Benevento and an independent Salerno. He became sole emperor on the death of his father in September 855. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-prum">Treaty of Prüm</a> gave him no territory outside Italy, which aroused his discontent. In 864 he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with an army to support archbishops whom Pope Nicholas I had deposed over the divorce of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a>, but fell ill and made peace with the pope.</p><p>Louis spent much of his reign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> who were ravaging southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 866 he routed them but lacked a fleet, so in 869 he allied with the eastern emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>, who sent ships, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Bari, fell in 871. Afterwards he quarrelled with Basil and defended his right to the title emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. In August 871 Adelchis, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, attacked him in his palace, robbed him and held him prisoner for a month, until new Aghlabid landings made Adelchis release him. Pope Adrian II crowned him a second time in May 872.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louis won further successes against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a>, relieving the siege of Salerno and driving them from Capua, but could not punish Adelchis. He died near Ghedi, in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a>, on 12 August 875, naming his cousin Carloman, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, as his successor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and was buried in the Basilica of Sant'Ambrogio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. He left two daughters, Gisela, abbess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> at Brescia, and Ermengard, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence">Boso of Provence</a>. The chronicler Andreas of Bergamo, the main source for his campaigns in the south, noted that after his death a great tribulation came to Italy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis II of Italy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Louis II of Italy</b>, Carolingian emperor, tried to replace him with his own favoured candidate, Benedict III was restored to the papacy by a popular uprising.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-29">29 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Lothair I and his son <b>Louis II</b> ruled Italy as emperors; Louis II spent decades fighting Arab raiders in the south and recovered Bari in 871.</blockquote><a href="/state/carolingian-kingdom-of-italy">Carolingian Kingdom of Italy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carolingian emperor <b>Louis II</b> besieged Bari for several years with Lombard allies and Byzantine naval help, taking the city in 871 and capturing Sawdan.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bari">Emirate of Bari</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles died childless in 863, and his brothers Lothair II and the emperor <b>Louis II</b> divided the kingdom between them.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-provence">Kingdom of Provence</a></li><li><blockquote>Shortly before his death in 855 Lothair retired to the abbey of Prüm and, by the Treaty of Prüm, divided the kingdom among his three sons: <b>Louis II</b> received Italy, Lothair II the north, which became the Kingdom of Lotharingia, and Charles the Kingdom of Provence.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Louis II</b> took Bari from the Arabs in 871, the Byzantines occupied it in 876 and, through the campaigns of Nikephoros Phokas the Elder in 885–886, recovered much of Apulia and Calabria.</blockquote><a href="/state/theme-of-longobardia">Theme of Longobardia</a></li></ul></section>
